dollar-lc.patch -- the project's "$LC" convention, written in the compiler's source.

tools/patch-toolchain.py (upstream's, dbalatoni13/nfsmw, 2026-08-10; profile prodg393-gc-cc1plus) patches the
official cc1plus.exe in the machine code: six calls and three pushes are redirected to helper code in a cave of
.text.  This is the same change in varasm.c, under #ifdef SN_DOLLAR_LC: constant-pool labels become local
SYMBOLS `$LCn`, each with `.type ...,@object` and `.size`, so every literal is a symbol of the object (the
assembler drops `.L` labels): objdiff pairs them with the original's data labels and config/GOWE69/keep.lst
can name them.  Without the macro, the stock `.LCn` labels are emitted.

Makefile.mingw compiles varasm.c twice and only cc1plus gets -DSN_DOLLAR_LC: the cc1.exe of the official
toolchain does not carry the binary patch either.  Measured: with this patch the cc1plus.exe built from source
gives the same assembly as the project's patched cc1plus.exe on all 409 v1.76 units of GameCube.

--- a/gcc/varasm.c
+++ b/gcc/varasm.c
@@ -142,6 +142,20 @@
 #include "real.h"
 #include "toplev.h"
 #include "dbxout.h"
+
+/* SN_DOLLAR_LC: the decompilation project's convention (tools/patch-toolchain.py, profile
+   prodg393-gc-cc1plus): constant-pool labels are local SYMBOLS $LCn with .type/.size, so that every
+   literal shows up in the object's symbol table.  Without it, the stock .LCn labels.  */
+#ifdef SN_DOLLAR_LC
+#define LC_GENERATE_LABEL(LABEL, NUM) sprintf (LABEL, "*$%s%d", "LC", (unsigned) (NUM))
+#define LC_OUTPUT_LABEL(FILE, NUM) \
+  (fprintf (FILE, "\t.type\t$%s%d,@object\n", "LC", (NUM)), fprintf (FILE, "$%s%d:\n", "LC", (NUM)))
+#define LC_OUTPUT_SIZE(FILE, NUM, SIZE) fprintf (FILE, "\t.size\t$%s%d,%d\n", "LC", (NUM), (int) (SIZE))
+#else
+#define LC_GENERATE_LABEL(LABEL, NUM) ASM_GENERATE_INTERNAL_LABEL (LABEL, "LC", NUM)
+#define LC_OUTPUT_LABEL(FILE, NUM) ASM_OUTPUT_INTERNAL_LABEL (FILE, "LC", NUM)
+#define LC_OUTPUT_SIZE(FILE, NUM, SIZE) ((void) 0)
+#endif
 #include "sdbout.h"
 
 #include "obstack.h"
